{
  "an4.11:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 4.11 ",
  "an4.11:0.2": "2. Walking ",
  "an4.11:0.3": "Walking ",
  "an4.11:1.1": "“Mendicants, suppose a mendicant has a sensual, malicious, or cruel thought while walking. ",
  "an4.11:1.2": "They tolerate it and don’t give it up, dispel it, eliminate it, and obliterate it. ",
  "an4.11:1.3": "Such a mendicant is said to be ‘not keen or prudent, always lazy, and lacking energy’ when walking. ",
  "an4.11:2.1": "Suppose a mendicant has a sensual, malicious, or cruel thought while standing … ",
  "an4.11:2.2": "",
  "an4.11:2.3": "",
  "an4.11:3.1": "sitting … ",
  "an4.11:3.2": "",
  "an4.11:3.3": "",
  "an4.11:4.1": "or when lying down while awake. ",
  "an4.11:4.2": "They tolerate it and don’t give it up, dispel it, eliminate it, and obliterate it. ",
  "an4.11:4.3": "Such a mendicant is said to be ‘not keen or prudent, always lazy, and lacking energy’ when lying down while awake. ",
  "an4.11:5.1": "Suppose a mendicant has a sensual, malicious, or cruel thought while walking. ",
  "an4.11:5.2": "They don’t tolerate it, but give it up, dispel it, eliminate it, and obliterate it. ",
  "an4.11:5.3": "Such a mendicant is said to be ‘keen and prudent, always energetic and resolute’ when walking. ",
  "an4.11:6.1": "Suppose a mendicant has a sensual, malicious, or cruel thought while standing … ",
  "an4.11:6.2": "",
  "an4.11:6.3": "",
  "an4.11:7.1": "sitting … ",
  "an4.11:7.2": "",
  "an4.11:7.3": "",
  "an4.11:8.1": "or when lying down while